<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Space exploration</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Space exploration is the physical investigation of outer space by uncrewed robotic space probes and through human spaceflight, made possible by large rockets in the mid-twentieth century. Its early era was driven by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/space-race">Space Race</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>: the Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sputnik-1">Sputnik 1</a> became the first human-made object to orbit Earth on 4 October 1957, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuri-gagarin">Yuri Gagarin</a> made the first human spaceflight aboard Vostok 1 in 1961, and the American Apollo 11 mission made the first Moon landing on 20 July 1969. From the 1970s the focus moved to reusable hardware such as the Space Shuttle and to cooperation, above all the International Space Station, built between 1998 and 2011.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/space-exploration.jpg" alt="Space exploration" width="330" height="323" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Project Apollo Archive · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AS11-40-5964_(21037459754).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Observation of objects in space predates reliable recorded history, and the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telescope">telescope</a> is said to have been invented in 1608 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> by the eyeglass maker Hans Lipperhey. Galileo Galilei made the first recorded astronomical use of one in 1609, and in 1668 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaac-newton">Isaac Newton</a> built the first fully functional reflecting telescope.</p><p>On 20 June 1944 a German V-2 rocket, launched in a test from Peenemünde, became the first human-made object to reach outer space, with an apogee of 176 kilometres. It did not reach orbital velocity and returned to Earth, making it the first sub-orbital spaceflight.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sputnik-1">Sputnik 1</a>, launched by an R-7 rocket on 4 October 1957, weighed about 83 kg and carried two radio transmitters whose beeps could be heard around the globe. The Soviet programme achieved many first milestones: the first living being in orbit in 1957, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuri-gagarin">Yuri Gagarin</a>'s flight aboard Vostok 1 on 12 April 1961, which completed one orbit in about 1 hour 48 minutes, the first spacewalk by Alexei Leonov on 18 March 1965, the first soft landing on another body, Luna 9 on the Moon on 3 February 1966, and the first space station, Salyut 1, on 19 April 1971.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo-11">Apollo 11</a> made the first crewed landing on another celestial body on 20 July 1969. Six spacecraft in all landed humans on the Moon, from 1969 to the last landing in 1972. Probes have also visited Venus, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> sent more landers than anyone, Mars, Jupiter, Saturn and the other planets, and Voyager 1 passed the heliopause into interstellar space on 25 August 2012.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>From the 1970s, effort moved from one-off flights to reusable hardware such as the Space Shuttle, and from competition to cooperation, above all the International Space Station, built between 1998 and 2011 and inhabited continuously since 2000. Valeri Polyakov's single spaceflight of almost 438 days aboard the Mir station has not been surpassed. The 2000s brought advances in the national programmes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and the 2010s the rise of a private space industry with its own launch vehicles and capsules.</p><p>Supporters justify the cost by pointing to scientific research, economic benefits such as NASA spin-offs, possible mining of asteroids and the inspiration of young people towards science and engineering. In the 2020s, the Chinese-led International Lunar Research Station and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artemis">Artemis</a> programme, each with international partners, were the two main programmes centred on the Moon.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Space exploration” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
